WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.156
Values that merge showed how the value
decides what concurrent writes mean.

2
00:00:05.156 --> 00:00:09.316
Here, we choose which keys to read,
and how to keep the walk going.

3
00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:14.814
This is Scans, filters and cursors:
ordered ranges, server-side predicates,

4
00:00:14.814 --> 00:00:16.187
and resumable pages.

5
00:00:17.187 --> 00:00:21.796
A streaming scan walks live keys
in strict lexicographic order.

6
00:00:22.146 --> 00:00:25.566
Give it a lower bound,
an upper bound, or both.

7
00:00:25.566 --> 00:00:29.349
The lower bound is included;
the upper bound is not.

8
00:00:29.699 --> 00:00:33.128
A reverse scan walks
that same range from high to low.

9
00:00:34.128 --> 00:00:38.809
A typed scan can also take a predicate:
a C-sharp expression over the value.

10
00:00:39.159 --> 00:00:43.126
The client translates that expression
to a small, serialisable rule.

11
00:00:43.126 --> 00:00:47.269
An unsupported expression fails before
any request reaches the cluster.

12
00:00:47.619 --> 00:00:51.494
Each leaf checks its own values
against their JSON document.

13
00:00:51.494 --> 00:00:53.173
Only matches are returned.

14
00:00:53.523 --> 00:00:57.461
A key-only scan sends no values
across the wire.

15
00:00:57.461 --> 00:01:01.727
An entry scan returns only
matching key-value pairs.

16
00:01:02.077 --> 00:01:04.344
The allowlist covers member access,

17
00:01:04.344 --> 00:01:08.556
constants, comparisons, boolean logic,
and ordinal string checks.

18
00:01:08.556 --> 00:01:10.240
Conversions are unwrapped,

19
00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:15.034
and expressions independent of the value
are evaluated once on the client.

20
00:01:15.384 --> 00:01:20.562
A method call on the value outside that
set is not silently sent to the server.

21
00:01:20.562 --> 00:01:21.545
It is rejected.

22
00:01:22.545 --> 00:01:26.788
For a long streaming walk,
use the resilient key or entry scans,

23
00:01:26.788 --> 00:01:28.909
not the raw enumeration methods.

24
00:01:29.259 --> 00:01:33.246
If the remote enumeration is reclaimed,
the resilient wrapper reconnects

25
00:01:33.246 --> 00:01:36.956
with the filter intact and continues
after the last key it yielded.

26
00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:38.578
Recovery is bounded.

27
00:01:38.578 --> 00:01:43.540
If attempts run out, it throws instead
of returning a short range as complete.

28
00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:47.378
When you need explicit pages,
open a cursor.

29
00:01:47.378 --> 00:01:49.442
It returns an opaque identifier;

30
00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:52.538
the server checkpoints
progress after each page.

31
00:01:52.888 --> 00:01:56.331
Keep that identifier
in your own durable state.

32
00:01:56.331 --> 00:02:00.727
A cursor can resume after a silo failover
or client restart.

33
00:02:01.077 --> 00:02:05.293
Ask for each page until HasMore is false,
then close the cursor.

34
00:02:05.643 --> 00:02:10.542
For a stable saga-decision view
across pages, open a point-in-time cursor.

35
00:02:10.542 --> 00:02:13.719
A live cursor can reflect
changes between pages.

36
00:02:15.519 --> 00:02:17.498
Next on Build: Atomic writes.

37
00:02:17.498 --> 00:02:21.455
The links are on this episode's page
in the documentation.

38
00:02:21.805 --> 00:02:24.852
Predicate operations
explains the filter language.

39
00:02:24.852 --> 00:02:28.692
The API reference gives each scan
and cursor method's contract.
